= Goodryke's Scheme Reaches a Dead End

Despite Oskar and Meissner’s willingness to entertain Goodryke’s schemes to end the political deadlock, Staal’s strengthened position has made it harder to instill a sense of urgency in DuFour—at least enough to convince him to do the impossible. Goodryke, meanwhile, refuses to abandon his demand for the chancellorship, arguing that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> are quickly collapsing and that he, not they, should be the senior partner.

A furious [+ royalist_leader +] has rejected Goodryke’s call for a meeting between his movement and the Camarilla, and negotiations have since collapsed. Although Goodryke’s desire for revenge against Staal remains undimmed, he increasingly sees [+ royalist_chancellor +]’s appointment as impossible—and himself as the only viable alternative. A belief that [+ royalist_leader +] refuses to believe in.

- @nazi_2

@nazi_2
title: The chancellor is safe, for now.
on-arrival: papen_goal = "Push for his own reappointment"; meissner_goal = "Reconcile Staal and DuFour"; oskar_goal = "Push for Goodryke's reappointment"

Goodryke's constant nagging remains a thorn in Staal's side, especially given that the former chancellor lives just meters from the President's residence—and with Oskar von DuFour continuing to influence his father.

Meissner, however, has come to a realization: Staal is here to stay. He is not driven by ideology—having served under Harden beforehand; he was simply trying to do what he believed was best for the nation—an aim that now means supporting Staal and his policies.